  "voiceover": "Who taught the world to care for the weak? For most of history, it wasn't obvious. Strength ruled, and the weak were spent. Unwanted infants were left out to die — and the best minds of the ancient world defended it. Aristotle wanted it written into law: no deformed child shall live. Then the Almighty did the unthinkable: God himself stooped to the very bottom of the human heap, and became one of them. Jesus tied his own identity to the powerless — whatever you did for one of the least of these, he said, you did for me. The hungry, the sick, the outcast stranger — this is where the King is found. So his followers cared for the sick as the sick, opened hospitals for the poor, and, as even the pagan emperor Julian complained when he tried to revive the old gods, cared for the poor who were not their own. The decency we now take for granted — that a society is judged by how it treats its weakest — was learned. And this is where it was learned: from the God of Israel who came down and made himself the friend of the powerless.",
